os-ctrld provides a native OPNsense GUI for configuring and running ctrld
(https://github.com/Control-D-Inc/ctrld), a DNS forwarding proxy, as the
client-facing DNS entry point alongside the existing Unbound and Dnsmasq
services.

Unlike Unbound, ctrld's policy engine can route DNS queries to different
upstream profiles based on the source network/VLAN, and can tag outgoing
DNS-over-HTTPS requests with per-client metadata (IP/MAC/hostname). This
plugin is built specifically to make that possible with NextDNS: separate
NextDNS profiles per VLAN, with individual devices distinguishable in
NextDNS's own dashboard and logs -- something that cannot be done with
Unbound's forward-zone model, since forward-zone selection in Unbound
cannot vary by source client or network.

In the resulting architecture, ctrld takes over the client-facing listener
role previously held by Unbound. Unbound is not removed: it is repurposed
to answer only *.in-addr.arpa reverse zones and the local "internal"
domain, on its existing non-default loopback port, exactly as it does
today for Dnsmasq. Dnsmasq continues to handle DHCP unchanged.

Why not just run multiple independent validating resolvers (Unbound or
PowerDNS Recursor instances), each statically bound to one interface with
a single fixed forward-zone to one NextDNS profile, instead of adding a
new plugin? That alternative was considered and deliberately not chosen.
It preserves full end-to-end DNSSEC validation and achieves per-VLAN
profile separation, but it structurally cannot achieve per-device
metadata tagging inside NextDNS's own dashboard, since that capability
inherently requires the dynamic per-query routing this alternative avoids
by design. It is also not a "just use what's already there" shortcut:
OPNsense's native Unbound plugin has no multi-instance support today (it
manages exactly one merged configuration via .opnsense.d includes), so
this alternative would itself require comparable new plugin
infrastructure to become GUI-manageable.

DNSSEC note: ctrld is a forwarding proxy and does not independently
validate DNSSEC locally. NextDNS does validate DNSSEC on its own
infrastructure and blocks responses with invalid signatures or on
zones that are not properly signed, so validation is not absent from
the pipeline -- it happens upstream instead of locally. This makes the
trust model hop-by-hop (trusting NextDNS's validation and infrastructure
integrity) rather than end-to-end (independently verifiable locally
regardless of any intermediate party). This is a real difference, but
not an unusual posture: most consumer encrypted-DNS tooling, including
AdGuard Home's typical configuration, works the same way. The plugin's
GUI surfaces this tradeoff persistently on the main configuration page
rather than hiding it in a tooltip.

Known prerequisite for upstream distribution: ctrld is not currently
available as a FreeBSD port and is only distributed as a manual binary
download from its GitHub releases. This plugin manages an
already-installed ctrld binary (the same posture dns/dnscrypt-proxy
takes toward its own port dependency) and does not fetch or vendor a
precompiled binary itself. Packaging ctrld as a proper FreeBSD port is
a separate prerequisite for a from-ports installation story.

